CHUNK({"chunk":"G","module":"m4","topics":[{"id":"m4-xrays","title":"X-rays: nature & production","short":"X-rays & production","blurb":"Where X-rays sit in the spectrum, who found them, how a tube makes them, and why the spectrum is a smooth hump with sharp Kα and Kβ spikes on top.","source":"Pages 25–29","tabs":[{"title":"Start here: what XRD is","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\"><b>XRD (X-ray diffraction)</b> is a way of finding out <b>how the atoms inside a solid are arranged</b>. You shine X-rays at a powdered sample, and it sends them back out only at certain angles. The list of those angles is a <b>fingerprint</b> that tells you what the solid is.</p>\n          <p>The slides use a lot of words before explaining them. Here they are, in plain language, before you meet them.</p>\n          <h3>Crystal words</h3>\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Word</th><th>Plain meaning</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td><b>Crystal</b></td><td>A solid whose atoms sit in a <b>neat pattern that repeats</b> over and over, like tiles on a floor. Salt, sugar, quartz and most metals are crystals.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Amorphous</b></td><td>A solid with <b>no repeating pattern</b>; the atoms are jumbled. Glass and most plastics.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Lattice</b></td><td>The repeating grid of points the atoms sit on.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Unit cell</b></td><td>The <b>smallest box</b> of atoms that, copied in every direction, builds the whole crystal. Like one tile. Its edge lengths (a, b, c) are the <b>lattice parameters</b>.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Lattice planes</b></td><td>Flat, evenly spaced sheets of atoms running through the crystal, like the pages of a closed book.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>d (d-spacing)</b></td><td>The <b>gap between two neighbouring planes</b>. Usually a few ångströms.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Miller indices (hkl)</b></td><td>Three numbers in brackets, like (111) or (200), that act as a <b>name for one set of planes</b>. You only need to read them as labels on peaks.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Phase</b></td><td>One particular crystal form of a substance. Quartz and cristobalite are two phases of SiO<sub>2</sub>. (Not the same as the <em>phase of a wave</em> below.)</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n          <h3>Wave and unit words</h3>\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Word</th><th>Plain meaning</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td><b>Å (ångström)</b></td><td>1 Å = 10<sup>−10</sup> m = 0.1 nm. Roughly the size of an atom.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>keV</b></td><td>Kilo-electron-volt, a unit of photon energy. 1 keV = 1000 eV.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>In phase / out of phase</b></td><td>Two waves are in phase when crest meets crest (they add up); out of phase when crest meets trough (they cancel).</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Monochromatic</b></td><td>Only <b>one wavelength</b>. XRD needs this.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>θ and 2θ</b></td><td>θ is the angle between the X-ray and the planes. The detector sits at 2θ from the incoming beam, so XRD graphs are plotted against <b>2θ</b>.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Diffractogram</b></td><td>The XRD graph: <b>intensity (y) vs 2θ (x)</b>, a row of peaks.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Coherent</b></td><td>Waves that stay in step with each other.</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n          <h3>The whole story in five lines</h3>\n          <ol class=\"steps\">\n            <li><b>Make X-rays</b> in a tube (this topic).</li>\n            <li><b>Shine them on a crystal.</b> Its planes are about as far apart as the X-ray wavelength (~1 Å), so they scatter it like a grating.</li>\n            <li><b>Waves from different planes add up</b> only at angles where their extra path is a whole number of wavelengths. That rule is <b>Bragg's law, 2d sin θ = nλ</b>.</li>\n            <li><b>Sweep the angle</b> with a diffractometer and record a peak at each such angle.</li>\n            <li><b>Read the peaks</b>: positions give d and the crystal type, width gives crystallite size (Scherrer equation).</li>\n          </ol>\n          <div class=\"note why\"><p>Why not just use a microscope? Atoms are ~1 Å apart, far smaller than visible light (400–700 nm) can show. X-rays have a wavelength about that size, so they can \"feel\" the spacing.</p></div>\n        "},{"title":"What X-rays are","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">X-rays are <b>energetic, high-frequency electromagnetic radiation</b>. On the spectrum they sit between UV and gamma rays.</p>\n          <div class=\"kv\">\n            <div><span>Wavelength</span><b>0.01–10 nm</b></div>\n            <div><span>Energy</span><b>1–100 keV</b></div>\n            <div><span>Frequency</span><b>3×10<sup>16</sup>–3×10<sup>19</sup> Hz</b></div>\n          </div>\n          <ul>\n            <li>Wavelength: <b>shorter than UV</b>, longer than gamma.</li>\n            <li>Energy: <b>higher than UV</b>, lower than gamma.</li>\n          </ul>\n          <div class=\"note why\"><p>These are one band written three ways. Short λ means high ν (c = λν) and high energy (E = hν). Quick check: c ÷ 10 nm = 3×10<sup>16</sup> Hz and c ÷ 0.01 nm = 3×10<sup>19</sup> Hz, exactly the frequency limits above.</p></div>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m4-xray-em-uses\"></figure>\n          <div class=\"note why\"><p>Why X-rays suit crystals: their wavelength is around 1 Å, about the same as the gap between planes of atoms. Visible light (400–700 nm) is thousands of times too long to pick those planes out.</p></div>\n        "},{"title":"Discovery & types","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\"><b>W.C. Röntgen</b> discovered X-rays in <b>1895</b> and was awarded the Nobel Prize in <b>1901</b>, the first Nobel Prize in Physics.</p>\n          <div class=\"kv\">\n            <div><span>Other names</span><b>X-radiation, Röntgen radiation</b></div>\n            <div><span>Known as</span><b>First example of ionizing radiation</b></div>\n            <div><span>First radiograph</span><b>Hand of Anna Bertha Ludwig, Röntgen's wife</b></div>\n          </div>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m4-first-radiograph\"></figure>\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Wavelength</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td>Hard X-rays</td><td><b>0.01–1 nm</b></td><td>Shorter λ, higher energy, more penetrating</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Soft X-rays</td><td><b>0.1–10 nm</b></td><td>Longer λ, lower energy, absorbed easily</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Analytical purpose</td><td><b>0.7–2 nm</b></td><td>The slice used for analysis such as XRD</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n          <div class=\"note why\"><p>Ionizing means an X-ray photon carries enough energy to knock an electron clean out of an atom, leaving an ion behind. That is why X-rays damage tissue and why labs shield them.</p></div>\n          <div class=\"note heads-up\"><p>The hard and soft ranges overlap on the page (0.1–1 nm counts as both). Most textbooks put the dividing line near 0.1–0.2 nm, about 5–10 keV: shorter is hard, longer is soft. Quote the page's ranges in the exam.</p></div>\n          <div class=\"note heads-up\"><p>\"0.7 to 2 nm\" for analytical work is almost certainly <b>0.7–2 Å</b> (0.07–0.2 nm). The anode lines used in XRD run from Mo 0.71 Å to Cr 2.29 Å, and Cu Kα is 1.54 Å, which is 0.154 nm.</p></div>\n        "},{"title":"The X-ray tube","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">An X-ray tube makes X-rays by <b>firing fast electrons at a metal target</b> and stopping them abruptly.</p>\n          <div class=\"kv\">\n            <div><span>Body</span><b>Evacuated glass/metal bulb</b></div>\n            <div><span>Cathode (−)</span><b>Tungsten (W), filament type</b></div>\n            <div><span>Anode (+)</span><b>Cu / Cr / Mo / W, disk type</b></div>\n            <div><span>Accelerating potential</span><b>25–150 kV</b></div>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"kv\">\n            <div><span>Cathode / anode</span><b>The negative and positive electrodes; electrons leave the cathode and fly to the anode</b></div>\n            <div><span>kV (kilovolt)</span><b>1000 volts; the bigger the voltage, the faster the electrons</b></div>\n            <div><span>Thermionic emission</span><b>A metal heated until it glows boils electrons off its surface</b></div>\n            <div><span>KE</span><b>Kinetic energy, the energy of motion</b></div>\n            <div><span>Atomic number (Z)</span><b>Number of protons in the nucleus (W = 74)</b></div>\n          </div>\n          <p>The cathode and anode are sealed in at opposite ends of the tube. The anode metal is chosen to suit the application.</p>\n          <ol class=\"steps\">\n            <li><b>Heat</b> The W filament is heated and gives off electrons by the thermionic process.</li>\n            <li><b>Accelerate</b> The 25–150 kV between cathode and anode drives them hard toward the anode.</li>\n            <li><b>Stop</b> The fast-moving stream hits the target and is rapidly decelerated.</li>\n            <li><b>Emit</b> The kinetic energy it loses comes out as X-rays, which leave through a window.</li>\n          </ol>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m4-xray-tube-schematic\"></figure>\n          <div class=\"note why\"><p>The bulb is evacuated so the electrons reach the anode without hitting gas molecules on the way. Only about 1% of their energy ends up as X-rays; the rest becomes heat, which is why the anode is water-cooled.</p></div>\n          <h3>Why tungsten</h3>\n          <div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">High atomic number, 74</span><span class=\"chip\">Efficient electron release</span><span class=\"chip\">Good thermionic emitter</span><span class=\"chip\">Made as thin wires</span><span class=\"chip\">M.P. 3422 °C</span></div>\n          <p>A filament has to glow white-hot for hours and keep giving off electrons. Tungsten melts at <b>3422 °C</b>, the highest of any metal, so it survives the job.</p>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m4-xray-tube-photos\"></figure>\n        "},{"title":"Continuous X-rays","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">Continuous X-rays, called <b>Bremsstrahlung</b> (braking radiation), are given off when a fast electron is <b>slowed down in the field of a nucleus</b>.</p>\n          <ol class=\"steps\">\n            <li><b>Approach</b> A fast electron passes close to a nucleus in the target.</li>\n            <li><b>Swerve</b> The positive nucleus pulls on it, bends its path and slows it, so it loses KE.</li>\n            <li><b>Emit</b> The loss of KE is made up by emitting an X-ray photon.</li>\n          </ol>\n          <div class=\"kv\">\n            <div><span>Interaction</span><b>Nucleus field</b></div>\n            <div><span>Cause</span><b>Electron deceleration</b></div>\n            <div><span>Output</span><b>Polychromatic, continuous, white</b></div>\n            <div><span>Discovered by</span><b>Nikola Tesla</b></div>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"note why\"><p><b>Polychromatic</b> means many wavelengths mixed together; <b>continuous</b> means no gaps between them; <b>white</b> is borrowed from white light, which contains every colour.</p></div>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m4-bremsstrahlung\"></figure>\n          <div class=\"note why\"><p>Why continuous: every electron takes a different path. A near miss loses a little energy and gives a low-energy photon; a close pass loses a lot and gives a high-energy one. Millions of electrons fill in every wavelength, so you get a smooth hump, \"white\" like white light. The only hard limit is at the top: a photon can't carry more than the electron's full KE, so the tube voltage sets the shortest wavelength.</p></div>\n          <div class=\"note heads-up\"><p>The page writes \"breaking radiation\". The German <em>bremsen</em> means to brake, so the usual English is <b>braking radiation</b>.</p></div>\n        "},{"title":"Characteristic X-rays","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">Characteristic X-rays come from an <b>inner-shell interaction</b>: a hole in an inner shell is filled by a higher-energy electron, and the energy gap leaves as one sharp X-ray line.</p>\n          <div class=\"note why\"><p>Electrons in an atom sit in <b>shells</b> at fixed energies. Counting out from the nucleus they are named <b>K</b> (innermost, n = 1, held tightest), then <b>L</b> (n = 2), then <b>M</b> (n = 3). An electron falling from an outer shell to an inner one gives up the energy difference.</p></div>\n          <ol class=\"steps\">\n            <li><b>Knock out</b> The incoming electron ejects a K-shell electron from a target atom.</li>\n            <li><b>Fill</b> A higher-energy electron drops into the vacancy, from the L shell or the M shell.</li>\n            <li><b>Emit</b> The energy difference between the two shells comes out as an X-ray photon.</li>\n          </ol>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m4-characteristic-emission\"></figure>\n          <div class=\"vs\">\n            <div class=\"side\"><h4>Kα</h4><p><b>L → K</b> electron drop. Smaller gap, longer λ, stronger line.</p></div>\n            <div class=\"side\"><h4>Kβ</h4><p><b>M → K</b> electron drop. Bigger gap, shorter λ, weaker line.</p></div>\n          </div>\n          <p>Characteristic X-rays were discovered by <b>Charles Glover Barkla</b> in <b>1909</b>.</p>\n          <div class=\"note why\"><p>Why \"characteristic\": shell energies are set by the charge on the nucleus, so every element has its own K, L and M gaps. The lines identify the anode metal, and they are sharp because each gap has one fixed value.</p></div>\n          <h3>Cu Kα, the lab standard</h3>\n          <div class=\"kv\">\n            <div><span>Energy</span><b>8.04 keV</b></div>\n            <div><span>Wavelength</span><b>1.5406 Å</b></div>\n            <div><span>Used for</span><b>Crystal structure study by XRD</b></div>\n          </div>\n          <p><b>K-lines</b> are the most useful in X-ray diffraction, and Cu Kα is the one most lab-scale instruments run on.</p>\n          <div class=\"note why\"><p>K-lines are the most intense lines in the spectrum, and diffraction wants one strong, single wavelength. The two Cu numbers agree: E = hc/λ = 12.4 keV·Å ÷ 1.5406 Å ≈ 8.05 keV, in line with the 8.04 keV quoted.</p></div>\n        "},{"title":"Spectrum & anodes","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">A real tube gives both kinds at once: a <b>smooth continuous hump</b> with <b>sharp characteristic lines</b> standing on it.</p>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m4-line-spectrum\"></figure>\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <thead><tr><th></th><th>Continuous</th><th>Characteristic</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td>Also called</td><td>Bremsstrahlung, white radiation</td><td>Line spectrum (Kα, Kβ)</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Interaction</td><td>Electron with the nucleus field</td><td>Electron with inner-shell electrons</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Spectrum</td><td><b>Polychromatic</b>, every λ</td><td><b>Sharp lines</b> at fixed λ</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Set by</td><td>Tube voltage</td><td>Anode element</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Discovered by</td><td>Nikola Tesla</td><td>C.G. Barkla, 1909</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n          <h3>Kα of common anodes</h3>\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Anode</th><th>Cr</th><th>Fe</th><th>Co</th><th>Cu</th><th>Mo</th><th>W</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody><tr><td>Kα (Å)</td><td>2.29</td><td>1.94</td><td>1.79</td><td><b>1.54</b></td><td>0.71</td><td>2.13</td></tr></tbody>\n          </table>\n          <div class=\"note why\"><p>Heavier element, bigger nuclear charge, inner electrons held tighter. The L → K drop grows and Kα gets shorter, which is the trend you see from Cr (Z = 24) through Fe, Co and Cu to Mo (Z = 42).</p></div>\n          <div class=\"note heads-up\"><p>W is listed as 2.13 Å. Tungsten (Z = 74) is much heavier than Mo, so its Kα has to sit well below 0.71 Å; the standard value is about <b>0.21 Å</b> (≈ 59 keV). 2.13 looks like a slipped decimal. Reproduce the page's list if it is asked for as written.</p></div>\n          <p>Try it: flip between Bremsstrahlung and Characteristic to see which part of the spectrum each one draws, then change the anode and watch the Kα line move.</p>\n          <div data-viz=\"xray-tube\"></div>\n        "}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Wavelength range of X-rays, and where that sits?","back":"<b>0.01–10 nm</b>: shorter than UV, longer than gamma."},{"front":"Energy and frequency range of X-rays?","back":"<b>1–100 keV</b> and <b>3×10<sup>16</sup>–3×10<sup>19</sup> Hz</b>."},{"front":"Who discovered X-rays, when, and when was the Nobel?","back":"<b>W.C. Röntgen, 1895</b>; Nobel Prize <b>1901</b>."}],"cardCount":12,"formulaCount":1},{"id":"m4-bragg","title":"Fundamental concepts in diffraction: Bragg’s law","short":"Diffraction & Bragg","blurb":"How waves bend and add up, why a crystal acts as a grating for X-rays, how ordered and disordered solids differ, and how two triangles give 2d sin θ = nλ.","source":"Pages 30–32, 37","tabs":[{"title":"Diffraction","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">Diffraction is the <b>bending of a wave</b> around a corner or through a gap. It happens when the slit or corner is <b>smaller than or equal to the wavelength</b>.</p>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m4-diffraction-door\"></figure>\n          <p>You see it every day with light bending around the <b>corners of doors and windows</b>.</p>\n          <div class=\"note why\"><p>A gap much wider than λ lets the wave go almost straight through. Shrink the gap toward λ and the wave fans out, because every point in the gap acts like a fresh little source.</p></div>\n          <div class=\"note why\"><p>For X-rays (λ around 1 Å) the gap has to be atom-sized. A crystal supplies exactly that: planes of atoms a few ångströms apart act as a natural grating. That is the whole basis of XRD.</p></div>\n        "},{"title":"Interference","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">Interference is the <b>interaction of two waves</b> that meet. They add, point by point.</p>\n          <div class=\"vs\">\n            <div class=\"side\"><h4>Constructive</h4><p><b>Reinforcing</b> interaction: the waves are in phase (crest on crest) and give a wave of greater amplitude.</p></div>\n            <div class=\"side\"><h4>Destructive</h4><p><b>Canceling</b> interaction: the waves are out of phase (crest on trough), cancel each other, and no net motion remains.</p></div>\n          </div>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m4-interference-waves\"></figure>\n          <p>Try it: drag the phase difference from 0 to 2π. At 0 the sum is twice as tall; at π it goes flat.</p>\n          <div data-viz=\"interference\"></div>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m4-young-double-slit\"></figure>\n          <div class=\"note why\"><p>What sets the phase is the <b>path difference</b>. If one wave travels a whole number of wavelengths (nλ) further, it arrives in step: constructive. Half a wavelength further and it arrives upside down: destructive. Bragg's law is this same rule for X-rays bouncing off crystal planes.</p></div>\n        "},{"title":"Crystalline vs amorphous","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">What XRD sees depends on how the atoms are packed. The microstructure of a solid comes in three kinds.</p>\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Arrangement</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td><b>Crystalline</b></td><td>The entire solid is made up of atoms in an orderly array</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Polycrystalline</b></td><td>Completely ordered, but only in segments</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Amorphous</b></td><td>No recognizable long-range order</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m4-microstructure-types\"></figure>\n          <h3>Same formula, different structures: SiO<sub>2</sub></h3>\n          <p>Quartz, cristobalite and glass all share the chemical formula <b>SiO<sub>2</sub></b>, but their crystal structures vary, and XRD shows it at once.</p>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m4-sio2-patterns\"></figure>\n          <div class=\"note why\"><p>A sharp peak needs the same plane spacing repeated again and again through the solid. Quartz and cristobalite have that, each with its own spacings, so each gives sharp peaks at its own 2θ. Glass has only short-range order, so the scattered waves never line up cleanly and you get one broad hump.</p></div>\n          <div class=\"note exam\"><p>A chemical analysis says \"SiO<sub>2</sub>\" for all three. A structural method like XRD is what tells quartz from cristobalite from glass.</p></div>\n        "},{"title":"Principle of XRD","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">XRD rests on one rule: <em>\"Every crystalline substance gives a pattern; the same substance always gives the same pattern; and in a mixture of substances each produces its pattern independently of the others.\"</em></p>\n          <ul>\n            <li>The XRD pattern of a pure substance works like a <b>fingerprint</b> of that substance.</li>\n            <li>It is based on the <b>scattering of X-rays by crystals</b>.</li>\n            <li>The atomic planes make the incident X-ray beam interfere with itself as it leaves the crystal. That is described by <b>Bragg's law</b>.</li>\n          </ul>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m4-xrd-mixture\"></figure>\n          <div class=\"note example\"><p>In the SiO<sub>2</sub> mixture above, the sharp cristobalite and quartz peaks stand on the broad glass hump. Each phase shows up on its own, exactly as the rule says, so XRD can pick out the parts of a mixture.</p></div>\n          <div class=\"note why\"><p>Why a fingerprint: peak positions come from the spacings between planes (set by the unit cell) and peak heights from which atoms sit where. Two different crystal structures practically never share both, so the pattern pins the substance down.</p></div>\n        "},{"title":"Bragg's law","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">X-rays reflected from neighbouring planes reinforce only when the lower ray's extra path is a whole number of wavelengths: <b>2d sin θ = nλ</b>.</p>\n          <p>The atomic planes of a crystal make an incident X-ray beam interfere with itself as it leaves the crystal. That is <b>X-ray diffraction</b>. Two parallel rays meet the planes at angle θ: one reflects at A on the top plane, the other at B on the plane below, a distance d further down.</p>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m4-bragg-geometry\"></figure>\n          <ol class=\"steps\">\n            <li><b>Path difference</b> The lower ray travels an extra δ = CB + BD.</li>\n            <li><b>Triangle ACB</b> sin θ = CB/AB = CB/d, so CB = d sin θ.</li>\n            <li><b>Triangle ADB</b> sin θ = BD/AB = BD/d, so BD = d sin θ.</li>\n            <li><b>Add</b> δ = d sin θ + d sin θ = 2d sin θ.</li>\n            <li><b>Condition</b> Diffraction occurs when that path difference equals nλ.</li>\n          </ol>\n          <div class=\"eq\" data-label=\"Bragg's law\">δ = 2d sin θ = nλ</div>\n          <div class=\"kv\">\n            <div><span>δ</span><b>Path difference between reflected waves</b></div>\n            <div><span>λ</span><b>Incident X-ray wavelength</b></div>\n            <div><span>n</span><b>An integer: 1, 2, 3…</b></div>\n            <div><span>d</span><b>Distance between lattice planes</b></div>\n            <div><span>θ</span><b>Angle between incident X-ray and the planes</b></div>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"note trap\"><p>θ is measured from the plane, not from the normal. The angle between the incoming and the diffracted beam is <b>2θ</b>, which is why diffractograms are plotted against 2θ. Halve the peak position before you use Bragg's law.</p></div>\n          <p>Try it: set d and λ, then sweep θ. The detector lights only when 2d sin θ / λ lands on a whole number; the n = 1, 2, 3 buttons jump straight there.</p>\n          <div data-viz=\"bragg\"></div>\n          <h3>Nobel Prizes in Physics</h3>\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>Who</th><th>For</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td><b>1914</b></td><td>Max von Laue</td><td>Discovery of X-ray diffraction</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>1915</b></td><td>W.H. Bragg and W.L. Bragg (father and son)</td><td>X-ray crystal structure analysis</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m4-nobel-xrd\"></figure>\n        "},{"title":"Worked problem","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">Bragg problems are one line of algebra: rearrange to <b>d = nλ / 2 sin θ</b> and keep the units straight.</p>\n          <p>X-rays of wavelength 0.9 Å are diffracted at an angle of 7° in the first order. What is the spacing between the adjacent planes of the crystal, in Å?</p>\n          <div class=\"worked\">\n            <div class=\"given\"><p>λ = 0.9 Å = 0.9×10<sup>−10</sup> m, θ = 7°, first order so n = 1, d = ?</p></div>\n            <div class=\"solve\">\n              <div class=\"eq\">2d sin θ = nλ</div>\n              <div class=\"eq\">2 × d × sin 7° = 1 × 0.9×10<sup>−10</sup></div>\n              <div class=\"eq\">2 × d × 0.1218 = 1 × 0.9×10<sup>−10</sup></div>\n              <div class=\"eq\">d = 0.9×10<sup>−10</sup> / 0.2436 = 3.6946×10<sup>−10</sup> m</div>\n            </div>\n            <div class=\"result\"><p>d = <b>3.69 Å</b> (3.6946 Å)</p></div>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"note heads-up\"><p>The page writes d = 3.6946×10<sup>−10</sup> <b>cm</b>. It should be <b>m</b>: 10<sup>−10</sup> m is 1 Å, which is how the next line gets 3.6946 Å. In cm it would be 0.037 Å.</p></div>\n          <div class=\"note exam\"><p>Shortcut: leave λ in Å and d comes out in Å directly, since sin θ has no unit. Using the full sin 7° = 0.12187 gives 3.692 Å; both round to 3.69 Å.</p></div>\n        "}],"flashcards":[{"front":"When does diffraction occur?","back":"When a wave passes a slit or corner <b>smaller than or equal to its wavelength</b>."},{"front":"Everyday example of diffraction on the page?","back":"Light bending around the <b>corners of doors and windows</b>."},{"front":"Constructive vs destructive interference?","back":"Constructive: waves <b>in phase</b>, greater amplitude. Destructive: <b>out of phase</b>, cancel, no net motion."}],"cardCount":10,"formulaCount":3},{"id":"m4-xrd-patterns","title":"Powder XRD: diffractometer and patterns","short":"Powder XRD","blurb":"The diffractometer in plain words (tube, sample, detector), why the detector sits at 2θ, how to read a peak, and what XRD is used for.","source":"Pages 33–34, 38","tabs":[{"title":"The diffractometer","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">A diffractometer is just <b>three things in a line</b>: something that makes X-rays, the powder sample, and something that counts the X-rays that bounce off. Everything else is helpers.</p>\n          <div class=\"vs\">\n            <div class=\"side\"><h4>1. X-ray tube</h4><p>Makes the X-rays.</p></div>\n            <div class=\"side\"><h4>2. Sample</h4><p>A flat layer of powder. Its crystals bounce X-rays off at certain angles only.</p></div>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"vs\">\n            <div class=\"side\"><h4>3. Detector</h4><p>Counts how many X-rays arrive at each angle.</p></div>\n            <div class=\"side\"><h4>Result</h4><p>A graph of counts vs angle (2θ), called a <b>diffractogram</b>.</p></div>\n          </div>\n          <h3>The idea in one line</h3>\n          <p>Turn the angle slowly. At most angles nothing comes back. At a few special angles (where <b>Bragg's law, nλ = 2d sin θ</b>, is true) the bounced waves add up, and the detector sees a spike. Those spikes are the peaks.</p>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m4-diffractometer-layout\"></figure>\n          <h3>The full list (for the exam diagram)</h3>\n          <p>Follow the X-rays from left to right. The helpers just clean the beam or the signal:</p>\n          <ol class=\"steps\">\n            <li><b>High-voltage supply</b> Powers the tube.</li>\n            <li><b>X-ray tube</b> Makes the X-rays.</li>\n            <li><b>Filter</b> Keeps one wavelength only (one λ, so each set of planes gives one sharp peak).</li>\n            <li><b>Collimator</b> Makes the beam thin and straight.</li>\n            <li><b>Sample</b> Bounces X-rays out at its Bragg angles.</li>\n            <li><b>Detector</b> Turns each X-ray into an electrical pulse.</li>\n            <li><b>Amplifiers</b> Make the pulses big enough to count.</li>\n            <li><b>Computer</b> Runs the scan and draws the diffractogram.</li>\n          </ol>\n          <div class=\"note exam\"><p>In the FAT, draw: <b>tube → filter → collimator → sample → detector → computer</b>, with the angle 2θ marked between the incoming beam and the detector. Then one line for each part, like the list above.</p></div>\n        "},{"title":"Goniometer & detector","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">The <b>goniometer</b> is the turntable that moves the sample and the detector together. That is all it does: it keeps the angles right.</p>\n          <ul>\n            <li>The sample tilts by <b>θ</b>.</li>\n            <li>The detector moves by <b>2θ</b>, twice as far.</li>\n          </ul>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m4-goniometer\"></figure>\n          <h3>Why 2θ and not θ?</h3>\n          <p>Think of a ball bouncing off a wall. It comes in at angle θ and leaves at angle θ on the other side, so it has turned by <b>2θ</b> in total. The detector has to sit where the bounced X-rays go, so it stays at 2θ. That is why every XRD graph is plotted against <b>2θ</b>.</p>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m4-theta-2theta-geometry\"></figure>\n          <h3>Two small parts you may see named</h3>\n          <div class=\"kv\">\n            <div><span>Göbel mirror</span><b>Shapes the X-ray beam and aims it at the sample (tube side)</b></div>\n            <div><span>Soller slit</span><b>A stack of thin plates that lets only straight rays reach the detector</b></div>\n          </div>\n          <h3>Detector types</h3>\n          <div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Gas-filled</span><span class=\"chip\">Scintillation</span><span class=\"chip\">Semiconductor</span></div>\n          <div class=\"note trap\"><p>In the Scherrer and Bragg formulas you use <b>θ</b>, which is half the 2θ read off the graph. Forgetting to halve it is the most common mistake.</p></div>\n        "},{"title":"Reading a diffractogram","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">The graph has angle (<b>2θ</b>) along the bottom and <b>intensity</b> (how many X-rays) up the side. Each peak tells you three things.</p>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m4-xrd-peak-parameters\"></figure>\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Look at</th><th>It tells you</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td><b>Where</b> the peak is (2θ)</td><td>The spacing between atom layers (d), through Bragg's law. The set of positions is the material's <b>fingerprint</b>.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>How tall</b> it is</td><td>How many crystals have that spacing. Taller = more of them.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>How wide</b> it is</td><td>How big the crystals are. <b>Wider peak = smaller crystals</b> (this is what the Scherrer equation uses).</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n          <h3>The three shapes to know</h3>\n          <div class=\"vs\">\n            <div class=\"side\"><h4>Crystalline</h4><p><b>Sharp, narrow peaks</b>. Big, well-ordered crystals.</p></div>\n            <div class=\"side\"><h4>Amorphous (glass)</h4><p><b>No peaks</b>, just one broad hump. The atoms have no repeating order.</p></div>\n          </div>\n          <p>Nanocrystals sit in between: the peaks are still there, but <b>broad</b>, because the crystals are tiny.</p>\n          <p>Try it: drag the crystallite size from 100 nm down to 2 nm and watch the peaks spread out, then switch on amorphous to see the hump.</p>\n          <div data-viz=\"xrd-pattern\"></div>\n          <div class=\"note heads-up\"><p>The same material gives peaks at different 2θ with a different X-ray source (Cu or Mo), because λ is different. Only compare patterns taken with the same source.</p></div>\n        "},{"title":"Database matching","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">To find out what an unknown powder is, you <b>compare its peaks with a library</b> of known patterns, like matching a fingerprint.</p>\n          <ul>\n            <li>Match the <b>positions</b> of the peaks first, then their <b>relative heights</b>.</li>\n            <li>If they line up with a library card, that is your compound.</li>\n          </ul>\n          <div class=\"kv\">\n            <div><span>JCPDS</span><b>Joint Committee on Powder Diffraction Standards: the main library of patterns</b></div>\n            <div><span>ICSD</span><b>Inorganic Crystal Structure Database: another library</b></div>\n          </div>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m4-nacl-pattern\"></figure>\n          <h3>Example: table salt (NaCl)</h3>\n          <div class=\"kv\">\n            <div><span>Structure</span><b>FCC (face-centred cube)</b></div>\n            <div><span>Cube edge</span><b>a = 5.64 Å</b></div>\n            <div><span>Tallest peaks</span><b>(200), then (220)</b></div>\n          </div>\n          <p>The numbers in brackets, like (200), are Miller indices: just labels for which set of atom layers made that peak.</p>\n          <div class=\"note trap\"><p>XRD tells you the <b>compound and its crystal form</b> (for example NaCl, or quartz vs cristobalite), not just which elements are present.</p></div>\n        "},{"title":"Uses, pros & cons","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">XRD is the cheap, everyday way to find out <b>what a solid is and how its atoms are arranged</b>.</p>\n          <h3>What it is used for</h3>\n          <ul>\n            <li>Telling <b>crystalline from amorphous</b> (sharp peaks vs a hump).</li>\n            <li>Identifying a compound by matching its pattern.</li>\n            <li>Finding the <b>size of the unit cell</b> and the crystal structure.</li>\n            <li>Finding the <b>crystallite size</b> (from peak width) and <b>strain</b> (stretched or squeezed layers).</li>\n            <li>Finding the <b>texture</b>: whether the grains point randomly or mostly one way.</li>\n          </ul>\n          <div class=\"vs\">\n            <div class=\"side\"><h4>Good</h4><ul><li><b>Cheap</b> and simple</li><li><b>Nondestructive</b>: the sample is not harmed</li><li>Works <b>in air</b>, no vacuum needed</li></ul></div>\n            <div class=\"side\"><h4>Bad</h4><ul><li><b>Weak for light elements</b> (H, Li, B): they have few electrons, and X-rays bounce off electrons</li></ul></div>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"note exam\"><p>For \"applications and advantages\" questions, the five uses and the four points above are enough.</p></div>\n        "}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does an X-ray diffractometer do?","back":"Produces <b>monochromatic X-rays</b>, focuses them on the sample, scans a 2θ range, detects reflected X-rays and their intensity, shows a <b>diffractogram</b>."},{"front":"Order of parts from tube to PC?","back":"HV supply → X-ray tube → <b>primary filter</b> → <b>collimator</b> → sample → detector & pre-amp → main amplifier → amplitude analyzer → control unit → communication module → PC."},{"front":"Three detector types?","back":"<b>Gas-filled</b>, <b>scintillation</b>, <b>semiconductor</b>."}],"cardCount":11,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m4-scherrer","title":"Powder XRD: crystallite size and numericals","short":"Crystallite size","blurb":"Crystallite vs grain vs particle and which instrument sees each, how peak broadening turns into a size through the Scherrer equation, and the p-XRD numerical worked through.","source":"Pages 35–37","tabs":[{"title":"Crystallite, grain, particle","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">Crystallite, grain and particle are three sizes of \"crystal\", one inside the next. The right order is <b>particle ≥ grain ≥ crystallite > unit cell</b>.</p>\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <thead><tr><th></th><th>What it is</th><th>Measured by</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td>Crystallite</td><td>A small crystal of many atoms/molecules; the portion of the crystal that diffracts light <b>coherently</b></td><td><b>XRD</b>, Scherrer's formula</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Grain</td><td>Forms during crystal growth from <b>multiple nucleation centres</b>; contains multiple crystallites</td><td><b>SEM</b></td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Particle</td><td>A single crystal or an <b>agglomeration of several grains</b>; grains combine to form particles</td><td><b>TEM</b></td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n          <p>Particle size is <b>always larger</b> than crystallite size.</p>\n          <div class=\"note why\"><p><b>Nucleation centre</b>: a spot where a crystal first starts to grow. Several start at once, each grows into a grain, and they meet at <b>grain boundaries</b>. <b>Agglomeration</b> means clumping together. <b>Coherently</b> here means the waves leave in step, because the lattice runs on without a break.</p></div>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m4-particle-grain-crystallite\"></figure>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m4-sem-grains\"></figure>\n          <div class=\"note heads-up\"><p>The page prints \"Particle ≤ grain ≤ crystallite < unit cell\", with every sign backwards. A particle holds grains and a grain holds crystallites, so it has to be <b>particle ≥ grain ≥ crystallite > unit cell</b>.</p></div>\n          <div class=\"note why\"><p>Why XRD only sees crystallites: X-rays diffract coherently only while the lattice runs on unbroken. A grain boundary or a defect resets the phase, so the size from Scherrer's formula is the size of those coherent blocks, not of the grain or the particle.</p></div>\n          <p>Try it: click particle, then grain, then crystallite to zoom in one level at a time, and note which instrument measures each.</p>\n          <div data-viz=\"size-hierarchy\"></div>\n          <div class=\"note trap\"><p>A small Scherrer size does not mean small particles. TEM can show particles far bigger than D, each built from many crystallites.</p></div>\n        "},{"title":"Scherrer equation","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">Small crystallites give broad peaks. The <b>Scherrer equation</b> turns that broadening into a crystallite size, D.</p>\n          <div class=\"eq\" data-label=\"Scherrer equation\">D = <span class=\"frac\"><span>kλ</span><span>β cos θ</span></span></div>\n          <div class=\"kv\">\n            <div><span>D</span><b>Crystallite size</b></div>\n            <div><span>β</span><b>FWHM in radians</b></div>\n            <div><span>θ</span><b>Bragg angle (half of 2θ)</b></div>\n            <div><span>λ</span><b>X-ray wavelength</b></div>\n            <div><span>k</span><b>Scherrer constant, depends on shape</b></div>\n            <div><span>k for FWHM, spherical, cubic</span><b>0.94</b></div>\n          </div>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m4-fwhm-peak\"></figure>\n          <p><b>FWHM</b> (full width at half maximum) is the width of the peak measured at half its height, I<sub>max</sub>/2, above the background.</p>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m4-peak-broadening\"></figure>\n          <div class=\"note why\"><p>Why small means broad: in a big crystal thousands of planes scatter. Move slightly off the Bragg angle and the tiny mismatch from plane to plane adds up until the waves cancel, so the peak is sharp. With only a few planes the mismatch never builds up to full cancellation, and intensity survives over a wider range of angles.</p></div>\n          <ol class=\"steps\">\n            <li><b>Halve 2θ</b> to get the Bragg angle θ.</li>\n            <li><b>Convert FWHM</b> to radians: degrees × π/180. (A <b>radian</b> is another angle unit: a full circle is 2π rad = 360°, so 1° ≈ 0.01745 rad.)</li>\n            <li><b>Match units</b>: λ in nm gives D in nm.</li>\n            <li><b>Plug in</b> D = kλ / β cos θ.</li>\n          </ol>\n          <div class=\"note trap\"><p>The two classic slips: leaving FWHM in degrees (D comes out about 57 times too small) and using cos 2θ instead of cos θ.</p></div>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m4-scherrer-portrait\"></figure>\n        "},{"title":"Worked problem","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">Estimate the crystallite size of a nanomaterial from its p-XRD peak. The whole job is <b>unit conversion, then one division</b>.</p>\n          <p>Peak position 2θ = 21.61°, FWHM of sample = 2.51°, k = 0.9 and λ = 1.5406 Å (degree to radian = degree × π/180).</p>\n          <div class=\"worked\">\n            <div class=\"given\"><p>2θ = 21.61°, so θ = 10.805°; β = FWHM = 2.51° = 0.043825 rad; k = 0.9; λ = 1.5406 Å = 0.15406 nm</p></div>\n            <div class=\"solve\">\n              <div class=\"eq\">D = <span class=\"frac\"><span>kλ</span><span>β cos θ</span></span></div>\n              <div class=\"eq\">cos 10.805° = 0.982257</div>\n              <div class=\"eq\">D = <span class=\"frac\"><span>0.9 × 0.15406</span><span>0.043825 × 0.982257</span></span></div>\n              <div class=\"eq\">D = <span class=\"frac\"><span>0.13865</span><span>0.043047</span></span></div>\n            </div>\n            <div class=\"result\"><p>D ≈ <b>3.22 nm</b></p></div>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"note heads-up\"><p>The page's working lists k = 0.94 and writes 0.94 × 0.15406 on top, yet reports 3.22 nm. That answer only comes from <b>k = 0.9</b>, the value in the question. With 0.94 the same numbers give <b>3.36 nm</b>. Use the k the question gives and keep it the same all the way through. (0.043825 rad also comes from π ≈ 22/7; with π = 3.1416 it is 0.043808 rad, same answer to three figures.)</p></div>\n          <p>Try it: load the Scherrer preset, then switch K between 0.9 and 0.94 to see where 3.22 nm and 3.36 nm come from. The Bragg side reruns the 0.9 Å, 7° problem from Bragg's law.</p>\n          <div data-viz=\"xrd-calc\"></div>\n          <div class=\"note exam\"><p>3.22 nm is well inside the nano range, which fits such a broad 2.51° peak. Write the formula, the converted θ and β, the substitution, then D with its unit.</p></div>\n        "},{"title":"λ from X-ray energy","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">Sometimes the question gives the <b>X-ray energy</b> (in keV) instead of the wavelength. Find λ first from <b>λ = hc/E</b>, then use it in the Scherrer equation.</p>\n          <div class=\"eq\" data-label=\"Wavelength from photon energy\">λ = <span class=\"frac\"><span>hc</span><span>E</span></span>, with E (J) = E (eV) × 1.602 × 10<sup>−19</sup></div>\n          <div class=\"worked\">\n            <div class=\"given\"><p>A crystalline material gives a diffraction peak at <b>2θ = 44.60°</b> with <b>FWHM = 0.95°</b>, using X-rays of energy <b>6.93 keV</b>. Take k = 0.9, h = 6.626 × 10<sup>−34</sup> J s, c = 3.00 × 10<sup>8</sup> m s<sup>−1</sup>, 1 eV = 1.602 × 10<sup>−19</sup> J. Find λ and the crystallite size D, and comment on it.</p></div>\n            <div class=\"solve\">\n              <p><b>Step 1: energy in joules.</b></p>\n              <div class=\"eq\">E = 6.93 × 10<sup>3</sup> eV × 1.602 × 10<sup>−19</sup> J eV<sup>−1</sup> = 1.110 × 10<sup>−15</sup> J</div>\n              <p><b>Step 2: wavelength.</b></p>\n              <div class=\"eq\">λ = <span class=\"frac\"><span>6.626 × 10<sup>−34</sup> J s × 3.00 × 10<sup>8</sup> m s<sup>−1</sup></span><span>1.110 × 10<sup>−15</sup> J</span></span> = <span class=\"frac\"><span>1.988 × 10<sup>−25</sup> J m</span><span>1.110 × 10<sup>−15</sup> J</span></span> = 1.79 × 10<sup>−10</sup> m</div>\n              <p>λ = <b>1.79 Å = 0.179 nm</b> (this matches Co Kα radiation).</p>\n              <p><b>Step 3: θ and β.</b></p>\n              <div class=\"eq\">θ = 44.60° / 2 = 22.30°, cos 22.30° = 0.9252</div>\n              <div class=\"eq\">β = 0.95° × <span class=\"frac\"><span>π</span><span>180</span></span> = 0.01658 rad</div>\n              <p><b>Step 4: Scherrer.</b></p>\n              <div class=\"eq\">D = <span class=\"frac\"><span>kλ</span><span>β cos θ</span></span> = <span class=\"frac\"><span>0.9 × 0.179 nm</span><span>0.01658 × 0.9252</span></span> = <span class=\"frac\"><span>0.1611 nm</span><span>0.01534</span></span></div>\n            </div>\n            <div class=\"result\"><p>λ ≈ <b>1.79 Å (0.179 nm)</b>; D ≈ <b>10.5 nm</b></p></div>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"note exam\"><p>Comment: 10.5 nm lies inside the 1–100 nm range, so the material is <b>nanocrystalline</b>. At this size a large fraction of atoms sit at surfaces and grain boundaries, so it has a high surface-to-volume ratio and properties (reactivity, melting point, optical and magnetic behaviour) that differ from the bulk. D is the crystallite size only; particles seen by TEM may be larger, built from several crystallites.</p></div>\n          <div class=\"note trap\"><p>Convert keV to J (× 10<sup>3</sup> × 1.602 × 10<sup>−19</sup>) before dividing. A handy check: λ (Å) ≈ 12.4 / E (keV), and 12.4 / 6.93 = 1.79 Å.</p></div>\n        "}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is a crystallite, and how is its size found?","back":"A small crystal of many atoms/molecules that <b>diffracts coherently</b>; size from the XRD peak via <b>Scherrer's formula</b>."},{"front":"How do grains form, and what sees them?","back":"From <b>multiple nucleation centres</b> during crystal growth; each holds many crystallites; measured by <b>SEM</b>."},{"front":"What is a particle, and what sees it?","back":"A single crystal or an <b>agglomeration of grains</b>; always larger than a crystallite; measured by <b>TEM</b>."}],"cardCount":9,"formulaCount":2}],"figures":[{"id":"m4-xray-em-uses","src":"m4/pages/p27.png","crop":[0.028,0.475,0.49,0.475],"caption":"Soft X-rays border the UV and hard X-rays run into the gamma region. Crystallography, mammography, medical CT and airport security each work in a different slice of photon energy.","alt":"Wavelength and photon-energy scale from visible light to gamma rays with soft and hard X-ray bands marked, and arrows to four example images: an X-ray crystallography spot pattern, a mammogram, a CT scan of a neck and an airport baggage scan."},{"id":"m4-first-radiograph","src":"m4/images/p27_12a73154.jpeg","crop":null,"caption":"The first radiograph of a human body: the hand of Anna Bertha Ludwig, Röntgen's wife. Bone and her ring show up because they absorb X-rays more strongly than flesh.","alt":"Old X-ray image of a hand with a ring on one finger, with small portraits of Anna Bertha Ludwig and Wilhelm Röntgen beside it."},{"id":"m4-xray-tube-schematic","src":"m4/images/p29_64a8e7c7.png","crop":null,"caption":"Heated cathode on the left, water-cooled anode target on the right, vacuum in between. The high voltage pulls electrons across, and X-rays leave where they hit the metal target.","alt":"Schematic of an X-ray tube: a heated filament cathode, electrons travelling right to an angled water-cooled anode, a high-voltage supply across the two, the vacuum inside, and a fan of X-rays leaving downward."},{"id":"m4-xray-tube-photos","src":"m4/pages/p29.png","crop":[0.345,0.54,0.375,0.44],"caption":"A recent sealed tube with its cathode, anode and X-ray exit marked, next to an old Crookes tube, the glass bulb type Röntgen-era X-rays came from.","alt":"Two photographs: a modern metal-and-glass X-ray tube with arrows labelling X-rays, anode and cathode, and an old egg-shaped glass Crookes tube with electrodes."},{"id":"m4-bremsstrahlung","src":"m4/images/p28_0897309d.jpeg","crop":null,"caption":"Electrons that pass close to the nucleus are bent hard and give off high-energy photons; distant ones lose little and give low-energy photons. Together they make the continuous spectrum.","alt":"Incident electrons passing a nucleus at different distances, bending by different amounts and emitting wavy X-ray photons labelled highest, medium and lowest energy, bracketed as continuous X-rays."},{"id":"m4-characteristic-emission","src":"m4/images/p28_d5a10600.jpeg","crop":null,"caption":"The incoming electron ejects a K-shell electron and scatters away; an outer electron falls into the hole and releases a characteristic X-ray photon.","alt":"Atom with nucleus and K, L, M shells; an incident electron knocks out an inner electron (ejected electron), the incident electron scatters, and an electron dropping inward emits a characteristic X-ray photon."},{"id":"m4-line-spectrum","src":"m4/images/p28_22b6423b.jpeg","crop":null,"caption":"X-ray intensity against wavelength: a smooth continuous hump with sharp K and L lines on top. The energy-level ladder on the right shows the electron drops into the L and K shells that make those lines.","alt":"Graph of X-ray intensity vs wavelength showing a continuous curve with sharp K and L peaks, a strip showing the X-ray spectrum with lines, and an atomic energy-level diagram with N, M, L and K levels and downward arrows."},{"id":"m4-diffraction-door","src":"m4/images/p30_6ad818ca.jpeg","crop":null,"caption":"Light spreading through a door opening into a dark room: the everyday face of diffraction around corners.","alt":"Dark room with a partly open door; bright light spills through the gap and spreads across the floor."},{"id":"m4-interference-waves","src":"m4/images/p30_a71dd75a.png","crop":null,"caption":"Two in-phase waves add into one taller wave; two out-of-phase waves cancel to a flat line.","alt":"Two sine waves added to give a larger sine wave, labelled constructive interference, and two opposite sine waves added to give a flat line, labelled destructive interference."},{"id":"m4-young-double-slit","src":"m4/images/p30_46a9816d.jpeg","crop":null,"caption":"Young's double slit: coherent laser light passes two slits, the spreading waves overlap, and the screen shows bright bands where they reinforce and dark bands where they cancel.","alt":"Coherent laser light hits a barrier with two slits; circular waves spread from each slit and overlap, with lines marking constructive and destructive interference, and a strip of red and dark fringes on the screen."},{"id":"m4-microstructure-types","src":"m4/images/p31_51b28890.jpeg","crop":null,"caption":"Crystalline: one regular array. Polycrystalline: ordered patches at different angles. Amorphous: no long-range order at all.","alt":"Three groups of red spheres: a perfectly regular stack labelled crystalline, patches of regular stacks meeting at angles labelled polycrystalline, and a random packing labelled amorphous."},{"id":"m4-sio2-patterns","src":"m4/pages/p31.png","crop":[0.645,0.345,0.325,0.4255],"caption":"Three forms of SiO₂. Quartz and cristobalite each give sharp peaks at their own 2θ values; glass gives only a broad hump.","alt":"Photos of quartz crystals, cristobalite and glass pieces beside their XRD patterns from 15 to 40 degrees two-theta: quartz with a tall sharp peak, cristobalite with a sharp peak at a lower angle, and glass with one broad hump."},{"id":"m4-xrd-mixture","src":"m4/images/p31_db13d725.jpeg","crop":null,"caption":"XRD pattern of an SiO₂ mixture: sharp crystalline peaks riding on the broad glass hump.","alt":"Green XRD pattern labelled mixture: a broad hump with two tall sharp peaks and a few small ones on top."},{"id":"m4-bragg-geometry","src":"m4/images/p32_a397e79b.png","crop":null,"caption":"Rays reflect at A on the top plane and at B on the next plane down. The lower ray's extra path is CB + BD, each equal to d sin θ. Top: a path difference of nλ gives a bigger wave; anything else cancels.","alt":"Two parallel (hkl) planes a distance d apart; incident wave front E and E-prime reflecting at A and B at angle theta toward reflected wave front F and F-prime; red lines from A to C and D on the lower ray, 2 theta marked at B, and small wave sketches for path difference equal to and not equal to n lambda."},{"id":"m4-nobel-xrd","src":"m4/images/p32_98fdfede.jpeg","crop":null,"caption":"Max von Laue (Nobel 1914), and W.H. Bragg and W.L. Bragg, father and son (Nobel 1915).","alt":"Three black-and-white portrait photographs of men in suits."},{"id":"m4-diffractometer-layout","src":"m4/images/p33_6d444e9b.jpeg","crop":null,"caption":"The beam path runs from the X-ray tube through the primary filter and collimator to the sample; the signal path runs from the detector through the amplifiers and control unit to the PC.","alt":"Block layout: high voltage supply feeding an X-ray tube, beam through a primary filter and collimator onto a ring-shaped sample, reflected into a detector and pre-amp, then main amplifier, amplitude analyzer, control unit, communication module and a PC."},{"id":"m4-goniometer","src":"m4/images/p33_1e957b11.jpeg","crop":null,"caption":"A lab diffractometer: X-ray tube and Göbel mirror on one arm, the sample at the centre of the goniometer circle, Soller slit and detector on the other arm at 2θ.","alt":"Cutaway drawing of a diffractometer with the X-ray tube and Gobel mirror on the left arm, a circular goniometer with the sample at its centre, and the Soller slit and detector on the right arm, with the angle 2 theta marked."},{"id":"m4-theta-2theta-geometry","src":"m4/images/p33_e3faee3b.jpeg","crop":null,"caption":"Fixed source, rotating specimen holder, rotating detector. Incident X-rays meet atomic planes 1 and 2 at θ, the diffracted beam leaves at 2θ, and the planes are d_hkl apart.","alt":"Semicircular goniometer with a fixed X-ray source on the left and a rotating detector on the right; incident and diffracted rays meet two atomic planes, with theta, 2 theta, d_hkl and d_hkl sin theta labelled above a rotating specimen holder."},{"id":"m4-xrd-peak-parameters","src":"m4/images/p34_4b80fb80.jpeg","crop":null,"caption":"A diffractogram recorded with Mo radiation. Read each peak for its position on the 2θ axis, its intensity (height) and its width.","alt":"XRD pattern of intensity against 2 theta from 10 to 30 degrees with Mo radiation, with arrows labelling peak intensity at the tallest peak, peak position on the axis and peak width on a peak near 25 degrees."},{"id":"m4-nacl-pattern","src":"m4/images/p34_50f24d26.png","crop":null,"caption":"Reference pattern of NaCl (FCC, a = 5.64 Å). Each peak carries the Miller indices of its planes; (200) is the strongest, then (220).","alt":"NaCl XRD pattern from 10 to 90 degrees two-theta with peaks labelled (111), (200), (220), (311), (222), (400), (331), (420), (422), (511), a ball model of the FCC cell, and cell data a = b = c = 5.64 angstrom, angles 90 degrees, density 2.14."},{"id":"m4-particle-grain-crystallite","src":"m4/images/p35_90191423.png","crop":null,"caption":"One particle (black outline) is split into grains (blue boundaries); inside a grain, the red block is a crystallite with column length D, built from planes a lattice spacing d apart.","alt":"Oval particle divided by blue lines into grains; one grain holds a red grid crystallite with a lattice spacing d, column length D and length L marked, and brackets on the right for grain size and particle size."},{"id":"m4-sem-grains","src":"m4/images/p35_85ad1257.jpeg","crop":null,"caption":"SEM image of a polished polycrystalline surface (10 µm scale bar). Each cell is a grain, and the lines between them are grain boundaries.","alt":"Grey SEM micrograph of polygonal grains separated by thin boundary lines, one boundary labelled, with a 10 micrometre scale bar."},{"id":"m4-fwhm-peak","src":"m4/images/p36_b5742353.jpeg","crop":null,"caption":"FWHM is the peak width at half the maximum height (I_max/2), measured above the background. The integral breadth is the other width measure, based on peak area.","alt":"Single bell-shaped peak of intensity against angle 2 theta, with the peak position 2 theta, I max, I max over 2, the FWHM arrow, integral breadth box, peak area and hatched background labelled."},{"id":"m4-peak-broadening","src":"m4/pages/p36.png","crop":[0.69,0.075,0.28,0.905],"caption":"A perfect, continuous lattice gives thin line peaks. Break it into smaller, slightly tilted blocks and the peaks at the same Bragg angles get wider.","alt":"Three stacked panels: a single regular grid above sharp line peaks, a grid with a slight break above narrow peaks, and a grid broken into several blocks above broad peaks, each plotted as intensity against Bragg angle."},{"id":"m4-scherrer-portrait","src":"m4/pages/p36.png","crop":[0.055,0.445,0.15,0.4],"caption":"P.H. Scherrer (1890–1969), who linked peak width to crystallite size.","alt":"Black-and-white portrait photograph of P.H. Scherrer with his name and dates below."}]});
